// The Enochian tablets in the Golden Dawn's version: the four
// Watchtowers, thirteen rows of twelve letters, and the Tablet of Union,
// four rows of five. Rows run from the top; a letter is the Latin
// transliteration, a capital where the source prints one. `marks` names,
// by row and column from 0, the squares that hold more: `alt`, the other
// letters the sources below print in that square (Regardie's first, the
// higher first, then the Equinox's and Greer's), and `reversed`, which
// says the grid's letter is printed reversed (never the `alt` letters).
//
// Water, Fire and the Tablet of Union follow Regardie, *The Golden
// Dawn*, vol. 4 (Aries Press, Chicago, 1940), the Book of the Concourse
// of the Forces: archive.org item
// IsraelRegardie-TheGoldenDawn-Vol4-1940, the Watchtowers of Water p.271
// (leaf n263) and Fire p.273 (n265), the Tablet of Union's lines p.275
// (n267). Where he prints two letters in a square the grid takes the
// higher, which he prefers while allowing both (p.306, n298).
//
// Nine squares read otherwise; rows and squares are counted here from 1.
// In eight, both other sources print another letter and the Order's
// names through the square read it too (the Seniors as the Greer
// edition's "The Four Elemental Weapons" names them; the names Liber
// Chanokh reads off Water, The Equinox, pp.235-236): Water's row 2
// square 3 (l: Olgota, Aalco), row 4 square 8 (l, where Regardie prints
// only i: Laoc), row 5 squares 7 (o: Laoaxrp) and 11 (s: Vssn) and row 6
// square 11 (o, which Regardie does not print: Rvoi); Fire's row 4
// square 7 (d: Alndvod), row 5 square 6 (o, where Regardie prints only
// a: Adoeoet) and row 13 square 7 (P: Arinnap; the Equinox and Greer
// print P, Regardie O over p).
//
// The ninth, Water's row 5 square 12, has no higher letter to take:
// Regardie prints four there, y and l side by side over N and h, so the
// rule cannot choose, and the grid takes N, one of his four, which both
// other sources print (reversed). That differs from Water's row 5 square
// 1 and row 12 square 1, where he prints two letters, one plainly higher
// (f over p, r over i): the higher stands there although both other
// sources print the lower, since no name settles them. Reversed letters
// are those the Equinox prints in Enochian and Greer mirrors.
//
// Cross-checked against The Equinox, vol. I no. 7 (London, 1912), "Liber
// Chanokh", Plates III and VIII (archive.org item
// crowley-equinox_202110, file "The Equinox Vol. 1, No. 7", leaves n241
// and n246), and the Watchtowers redrawn in Regardie, *The Golden Dawn*,
// 7th edition, ed. John Michael Greer (Llewellyn, ISBN
// 978-0-7387-4399-8), its colour plates "Enochian Tablet: The First
// Watchtower" to "The Fourth Watchtower". Plan 061 (Stage G notes) lists
// every square where they differ. Earth and Air were here before, with
// no source of their own. They differ from the Golden Dawn's in thirteen
// squares each; a comparison with a modern transcription of Dee's table
// (Sloane MS 3191) suggests they follow it. They stay so until the owner
// decides (plan 061).
{
  earth: {
    id: "earth",
    kind: "watchtower",
    grid: [
      ["b", "O", "a", "Z", "a", "R", "o", "P", "h", "a", "R", "a"],
      ["v", "N", "n", "a", "x", "o", "P", "S", "o", "n", "d", "n"],
